ttps://greenteapeng.bandcamp.com","https://soundcloud.com/greentea-peng-108510205","https://youtube.com/c/GreenteaPeng","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greentea_Peng"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/858/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"858","image":"https://i.discogs.com/zpjWp3zBvYH53wL6F1uc_nfXXg96QlRNLQKfhZCA5PI/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:587/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTg1OC0x/NjUzOTI1MTU0LTgz/NjcuanBlZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/Y7o-3QLN4rSEAqqbbvEo9YFidYyup6BCK6s_kvhmfGQ/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTg1OC0x/NjUzOTI1MTU0LTgz/NjcuanBlZw.jpeg","profile":"German acid/techno and tech house duo - formed 1991.\r\nWell known for distinctive acid sound credited to their virtuoso skill at manipulating Roland TB-303 bass synthesizers. They reputedly use up to six of these machines at once, some of which are highly modified.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:858","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":23017,"name":"Da Damn Phreak Noize Phunk"},{"id":70795,"name":"Techno Trash"},{"id":82202,"name":"Low Budget"}],"groups":null,"members":[{"active":true,"id":4864,"name":"Oliver Bondzio"},{"active":true,"id":13665,"name":"Ramon Zenker"}],"namevariations":["HRDFLR","HRDFLR.","Hard Floor","Harfloor","Hrdflr."]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/858-Hardfloor","type":"artist","urls":["http://hrdflr.com","http://hardfloor.bandcamp.com","http://www.facebook.com/hardfloor.de","http://www.instagram.com/hardfloor_official","http://linktr.ee/hardfloor_official","http://soundcloud.com/hardfloor","http://www.tursa.franken.de/Hardfloor_discog.html","http://x.com/Hardfloor303","http://vimeo.com/hardfloor","http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardfloor","http://www.youtube.com/user/HRDFLR"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/9400483/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"9400483","image":"","imageThumb":"","profile":"","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:9400483","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/9400483-Mesaelechtele","type":"artist","urls":["https://mesaelechtele.bandcamp.com/"]}},"/api/discogs/label/1866/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1866","image":"https://i.discogs.com/JuO51-lZvasOtw8-yLUjsen-4O17uPH1A9SILCO-lG4/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:300/w:299/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTE4NjYt/MTIzMzE5MzU1Ny5q/cGVn.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/eihJ8-awVKNAAX1er2SAHM24yPP_6lmJn1nApp8hh-4/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTE4NjYt/MTIzMzE5MzU1Ny5q/cGVn.jpeg","profile":"Columbia Records is the oldest brand name in recorded sound. Founded in January 1889 in Washington DC as the [l112015] by Edward D. Easton, the company first sold phonograph cylinders and later disc records.\r\n\r\n[u]For 78s that have \"Exclusive Artist (At Pressing)\" on labels add \"Exclusive Artist\" in the release notes and/or Free Text field (see [g6.1.5]).[/u] \r\n[b]For all unofficial/bootleg copies of this label please use [l149411].[/b]\r\n\r\nIt opened a British operation shortly afterwards. This was sold in 1922 to its own management, and then the independent [l=Columbia Graphophone Company Ltd.] itself purchased its almost bankrupt former parent in 1925. This was then merged with [l=The Gramophone Co. Ltd.], which had been set up in the UK in 1896. This company was incorporated as [l293204] (EMI) in 1931. US anti-trust laws forced the UK company to divest itself of its US operations that year.\r\n\r\nThe label was introduced in Japan in 1931 by the Nippon Phonograph Co. (also known as [l630804]) which was a subsidiary of [l293204] In 1935 EMI sold its stake in Nipponophone, but Nipponophone kept ownership of the Columbia brand for Japan. After World War II, the label became part of [l=Nippon Columbia Co., Ltd.]\r\n\r\nFor the next few decades the Columbia imprint (trademark) was exclusively owned by Columbia Graphophone Co. Ltd., outside North America, Japan, and Spain, where the rights were owned by a company unrelated to Electric & Musical Industries Ltd., [l=Discos Columbia, S.A.] (it was eventually sold to BMG Spain through RCA). In the US and Canada, Columbia was owned by [l=CBS Inc.]\r\n\r\nOn July 28, 1958, Columbia released its first stereo discs retailing for $5.98.  Earlier in June 1958, they had started selling mono & stereo phonographs.\r\n\r\nIn 1961, Columbia set up a parent company for itself called [l=CBS Records], which was a division of CBS. In 1965, [l97841] was merged into [l253617], with the rights to the Columbia trademark subsequently being reassigned to that company.  Around August 1969, Columbia finally entered the cassette tape market.  In January 1973, [l253617] began phasing out some of its domestic labels ([l2294], [l39354], etc.) including Columbia, in favour of the newly established EMI label, and subsequently no longer using the label for releasing pop or rock acts in much of the world (although it remained in use in parts of Asia and for non-pop releases).\r\n\r\nOn November 2, 1989, CBS sold its recording music distribution and label group to the Japanese conglomerate [l353657] for $400 million. On October 15, 1990, [l63404] (successor of [l253617]) officially sold its international rights to the Columbia trademark to Sony which rebranded CBS to Columbia on 1 January 1991. On April 17, 1991, Sony's acquisition of Columbia Records and affiliated labels was completed; CBS Records was renamed to [l=Sony Music Entertainment] and the Japanese branch [l=CBS/Sony], which was formed as a venture in 1968 between [l=CBS Inc.] and Sony, was rebranded as [l=Sony Records] (now [l=Sony Music Japan]). In Spain, Sony managed to acquire the rights from [l1003], which it had merged with on August 5, 2004 as [l53569]. However, since the trademark was not owned by an EMI affiliate in Japan, Sony was not able to acquire it for that market. This is why Sony can't use the original Columbia brand nor name in Japan, where the label was operated by [l213761] until October 1, 2002, when the name was changed to [l213791] On October 1, 2010, the name was changed back to [l213761].\r\n\r\nToday, Columbia (walking eye) remains one of the four flagship music labels operated by Sony Music Entertainment worldwide except in Japan, where Columbia (magic notes) is the primary imprint of Nippon Columbia.\r\n\r\n[b][u]WARNING[/u][/b]: Codes with the following prefixes should not be entered as catalogue number: XSS, ZSS, AL, BL. These codes are matrix numbers unique for each side of a vinyl release and should be entered in the Barcode and Other Identifiers section. The actual catalogue number is usually printed above the matrix number.\r\n\r\nEuropean releases in the 1980s until the late 1990s often had supplementary numbers printed on the release in the following format:\r\nxx-xxxxxx-xx (e.g.: 01-497424-10) in addition to short catalogue number 497424. These are not distribution codes or catalogue numbers but were added by the Dutch [l=CBS, Haarlem] / [l=Sony/CBS, Haarlem] plants and are commonly known as Computer Numbers. Do not add them as catalogue numbers. You can add these codes as \"Other\" in the BaOI section with \"Manufacturing code\" as description.\r\n\r\nCatalogue letters from 1989 to 2005 are listed as \"CK 12345\".\r\nLabel code: LC 0162 / LC 00162.\r\nHas the Spanish series [L=Espectacular (3)].\r\n\r\nFrench Columbia shellac codes, sizes, label colours, price codes (1951):\r\nBF - 25 cm (10\") - brown label (price code Medium)\r\nBFX - 30 cm (12\") - brown label (price code Medium)\r\nDF - 25 cm (10\") - black label (price code Standard)\r\nDFX - 30 cm (12\") - black label (price code Standard)\r\nGF - 25 cm (10\") - green label (price code Artistique)\r\nGFX - 30 cm (12\") - green label (price code Artistique)\r\nLF - 25 cm (12\") - blue label (price code Artistique)\r\nLFX - 30 cm (12\") - blue label (price code Artistique)\r\nRFX - 30 cm (12\") - red label (price code Medium)\r\n9000 to 11672 - 30 cm (12\") - black label (price code Standard)\r\n\r\nPlease note some mid-1950s (USA) 45rpm releases have on the label a release # which ends with -c which indicates \"country\":\r\n4-41008-c (year released 1957) [r=3374163]\r\n4-41012-c (year released 1957) [r=5144473]\r\n4-41020-c (year released 1957) [r=12487985]\r\n\r\nNote some releases from 1986 to 1991 have the ⎊ upside-down triangle (which looks like a Fallout Shelter) on the label which marks this era of pressing. Found on [l442751] and also [l447694] on LPs or 12\" singles, it helps designate the A side. This symbol also appears on some CDs and cassette tapes.\r\n\r\n[b]North American price codes on Columbia:[/b]\r\nKC - in the 1960s, also with KCL (mono) or KCS (stereo): $5.98 list price\r\nC - no barcoded cover: 1970 $4.98 list price\r\nPC - no barcoded cover: 1973 $6.98 list price\r\nJC - no barcoded cover: late 1976: $7.98 list price\r\nFC - 1978: $8.98 list price\r\nPC - barcoded cover: 1980 $5.98 and afterwards: $6.98\r\nTC - 1981: $8.98 list price\r\nQC - 1983: $8.98 list price\r\nJC - barcoded cover: 1983: $8.98 list price\r\nC - barcoded cover: 1983 $5.98 list price\r\nOC - barcoded cover: 1986: $10.98 list price\r\n\r\nA cassette adds a T.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:1866","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":29073,"name":"Sony Music Entertainment "},"sublabels":[{"id":2369182,"name":"\"BTA\" Series"},{"id":2369224,"name":"\"BTB\" Series"},{"id":2369227,"name":"\"BTD\" Series"},{"id":3477043,"name":"\"CAT\" Series"},{"id":3477070,"name":"\"CBT\" Series"},{"id":3477022,"name":"\"CDT\" Series"},{"id":735322,"name":"16 Most Requested Songs"},{"id":820330,"name":"2 On 1 Country"},{"id":599776,"name":"2 Αυθεντικά Album Σε 1 CD"},{"id":3244313,"name":"20 All-Time Great Recordings In One Great Package"},{"id":1728041,"name":"5th Anniversary Special"},{"id":1298409,"name":"A Hot Jazz Classic"},{"id":832407,"name":"A Jazz Ma